WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Take a thin thigh muscle in an anesthetized cat.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.420
Only about 15 percent of arterial pressure is spent crossing its capillary bed.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.800
Yet capillaries are its narrowest vessels.

4
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.320
So where does the rest go?

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:20.713
One rule runs everything here.

6
00:00:22.053 --> 00:00:23.833
Blood flows from high pressure to low.

7
00:00:25.113 --> 00:00:30.153
Through any stretch of vessel, flow equals the pressure drop across it, divided by

8
00:00:30.153 --> 00:00:31.153
its resistance.

9
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:37.473
Follow blood through the muscle: a small artery, an arteriole, many capillaries, a

10
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:43.093
venule, a vein. They lie end to end, so the same flow passes every segment.

11
00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:48.473
Then each segment's share of the pressure drop equals its share of the resistance.

12
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.913
Here are made-up round numbers, chosen so the capillaries match the cat.

13
00:00:55.353 --> 00:00:58.513
90 mmHg drive blood across the bed.

14
00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:02.393
The arterioles spend about 60 percent of it.

15
00:01:03.493 --> 00:01:05.093
The capillaries spend about 15.

16
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:08.057
Why so little in the capillaries?

17
00:01:09.397 --> 00:01:12.397
One capillary resists more than one arteriole.

18
00:01:13.537 --> 00:01:18.277
But each is short, and so many lie side by side that together they resist less.

19
00:01:19.577 --> 00:01:22.097
In muscle, that is a measured fact, not a law.

20
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:29.790
Now pull back. Most organs are fed side by side from the aorta and drain side by

21
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:35.890
side. So each sees nearly the same driving pressure, from aorta to right atrium.

22
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:38.433
Here is the one idea.

23
00:01:39.693 --> 00:01:43.933
The heart and the reflexes hold mean arterial pressure nearly steady.

24
00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:50.433
Each tissue then sets its own flow, by setting the resistance of its own arterioles.

25
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:55.667
Arterioles can do this because their walls are rings of smooth muscle.

26
00:01:56.907 --> 00:02:01.847
And by Poiseuille's law, resistance goes as one over the radius to the fourth power.

27
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.397
Let the muscle relax its arterioles.

28
00:02:06.357 --> 00:02:09.637
It takes more flow, and the body's total resistance falls.

29
00:02:10.977 --> 00:02:14.837
What happens to every other bed depends on whether the pressure is held.

30
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.520
But how far must they open?

31
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.620
To halve their resistance, how much wider must the arterioles get?

32
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.800
Twice as wide? Or much less?

33
00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:39.427
Much less. About 19 percent wider doubles the radius to the fourth power, so

34
00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:40.427
resistance halves.

35
00:02:41.687 --> 00:02:45.267
A small change in radius makes a large change in resistance.

36
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.000
So in muscle, most of the pressure is spent where the taps are.

37
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:56.040
Hold that pressure steady, and each tissue's own arterioles decide how much blood it

38
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:56.280
gets.
